WEBVTT

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:36.601
This week's pick is a fifty-three pound embroidered jumper from Sara's Threads, and the episode is about how machine embroidery works and why it suits some garments and ruins others. The most time-sensitive item is the closing-down sale at Watson and Wolfe, which is still taking orders with no published last-order date.

00:00:36.601 --> 00:00:40.547
It is a thin week for new products, so this is a shorter episode.

00:00:46.547 --> 00:00:58.921
A sewing machine makes a seam by interlocking two threads. An embroidery machine uses the same stitch, but a computer moves the fabric under the needle so the stitches build an image instead of joining two pieces.

00:00:59.571 --> 00:01:16.957
The design is a file in which a person, called a digitiser, has decided the stitch type, direction, length and density for every area. Three stitch types do most of the work. A running stitch draws a line. A satin stitch is a tight zigzag that makes a narrow raised column, used for outlines and lettering.

00:01:16.957 --> 00:01:21.992
A fill stitch, often called tatami, lays rows of short stitches to cover an area.

00:01:22.642 --> 00:01:39.005
Density is the part that decides whether a garment survives. Every stitch is a needle hole, and every thread pulls the fabric inward. Pack a large area densely onto a light cotton jersey and the cloth puckers around the design, goes stiff, and eventually tears along the line of holes.

00:01:39.005 --> 00:01:58.375
Makers manage this with a stabiliser, a backing sheet that holds the fabric flat while it is stitched, and by spacing the stitches out. This is why embroidery sits better on a heavy, stable fabric, such as a mid-weight fleece-back jumper, than on a thin tee. It is also why small, open designs wear better than a solid block of colour on a chest.

00:01:59.025 --> 00:02:14.791
Embroidery also changes how the garment is cared for. The stitched area does not stretch the way the knit around it does, so a tee that shrinks or relaxes in the wash will distort the surroundings of the motif. Turning the garment inside out for washing protects the thread face.

00:02:14.791 --> 00:02:19.975
The backing on the inside can scratch, which is why some makers add a soft cover patch.

00:02:20.625 --> 00:02:39.931
The vegan angle is the thread and the backing. Most embroidery thread is polyester or rayon, which is fine. Cotton and metallic threads exist too. Silk thread exists as a premium option, and some stabilisers and patches use wool felt. So a vegan label on the garment does not by itself say anything about what is stitched onto it.

00:02:39.931 --> 00:02:47.014
A maker who embroiders onto a bought-in blank, as the brand we will meet shortly does, controls the thread but not the fabric.

00:02:53.014 --> 00:03:10.401
One idea this week. A graphic or motif has a scale, and that scale is read against your body rather than against the garment. A palm-sized motif on a broad chest reads as a small detail. The same motif on a narrow frame reads as a statement. Nothing about the design changed; the ratio did.

00:03:11.051 --> 00:03:28.395
Try this. Take a tee or jumper you own that carries a print or embroidery. Stand in front of a mirror and note how wide the motif is compared with the width of your chest at the same height: roughly a quarter, a third or half. Then do the same with a plain garment where you judge the proportion right, and compare the two.

00:03:28.395 --> 00:03:38.677
Most people find their preference sits in a narrow band, and knowing it makes shopping faster, because product photos on a model of a different build mislead on exactly this ratio.

00:03:39.327 --> 00:03:58.719
The conventions on this are contested. One view holds that a motif should be centred on the chest, or sit just left of centre, because that is where the eye lands. Another holds that off-centre, low or oversized placement is more interesting precisely because it is awkward, and streetwear leans on it. The first view is about looking composed.

00:03:58.719 --> 00:04:08.469
The second is about looking considered but unconventional. Neither is right; they serve different aims, and the mirror comparison tells you which one you are actually after.

00:04:14.479 --> 00:04:21.007
The shopping is thin this week, so what follows is limited to items that could be properly confirmed. Two things matter.

00:04:21.657 --> 00:04:33.945
Watson and Wolfe's closing-down sale is still live and still taking orders, with no published last-order date. Anything you want from there should be bought on the assumption that returns and repairs will not be there later.

00:04:34.595 --> 00:04:44.109
And the Levi loafer from N A E, last month's pick, now shows sold out in every size on its product page, so that recommendation cannot currently be acted on.

00:04:50.109 --> 00:05:00.435
Nothing from this week is genuinely new and cleared. The one clean find is the Sara's Threads Shroom jumper, fifty-three pounds, which has been in the range since twenty twenty-four.

00:05:01.085 --> 00:05:17.042
Its page states eighty per cent cotton and twenty per cent recycled polyester, in Military Green, with every size from small to triple X L available, and the brand describes it as cruelty-free and vegan-friendly. It is a crewneck jumper with an embroidered mushroom.

00:05:17.042 --> 00:05:22.439
UK shipping is free over seventy pounds, so at fifty-three pounds you would pay for delivery.

00:05:28.439 --> 00:05:38.551
Watson and Wolfe's closing-down sale is the one deal with real stakes. The Trifold Chain Wallet is thirty-nine pounds, down from sixty-five, in chestnut brown and black.

00:05:38.551 --> 00:05:57.495
The page says it is made from an Italian corn-based alternative to leather, lined with fabric woven from one hundred per cent recycled plastic bottles, with no P V C, and ethically handmade in Istanbul. The brand's own About page says its factories are in Portugal and Turkey. The brown version showed forty-eight in stock.

00:05:58.145 --> 00:06:08.535
That is the brand's own account of its ethics, and no third party is cited. And because the brand is winding down with no end date given, there is no aftersales to count on.

00:06:09.185 --> 00:06:16.758
Black Friday banners are up at several brands, none with a stated end date. Treat the urgency as marketing rather than a deadline.

00:06:22.758 --> 00:06:41.894
Sara's Threads is a one-person, queer-owned London brand that machine-embroiders slogans and illustrations onto tees, jumpers, hats, socks and tote bags. Tees are twenty-eight or thirty-two pounds and jumpers fifty-three. It also runs a Pride collection, called Queen and Kings, and has a shop on Brick Lane that takes custom embroidery orders.

00:06:41.894 --> 00:06:45.862
The size range is inclusive, up to triple X L on the jumper.

00:06:46.512 --> 00:06:59.803
What is good about it: the work is hers, the prices are low for embroidered garments, returns are thirty days on unworn items, and it is one of the few brands where you can see the maker. The Shroom jumper's page states its fibres.

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:17.093
The weaknesses are real. The garments are wholesale blanks, which the brand says come from A S Colour and S F Clothing. So what you are buying is a stranger's fabric with her embroidery on it, and the ethics of that fabric are not hers to vouch for. She makes everything herself, so dispatch takes about a week.

00:07:17.093 --> 00:07:32.111
The organic fabric claim on the site is not supported on the one product page that could be read, which states cotton and recycled polyester without the word organic. The Queen and Kings pieces are limited editions in small runs, so availability is thin.

00:07:38.121 --> 00:07:56.980
Little has changed since September. Harlem's Fashion Row held its nineteenth annual show and style awards on the eighth of September during New York Fashion Week, and Wisdom Kaye received the L V M H presented Virgil Abloh Award there. The organisation also debuted a zine, called H F R Z, and gave a new Breakthrough Award.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:17.380
The one thing to buy is the Sara's Threads Shroom jumper at fifty-three pounds. It is not exciting and it is not new, but it is the only item this week whose own page states a fibre blend with no animal content, and there is no deadline pushing you.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:31.193
Nothing that passed the checks looks like a bad buy. The Watson and Wolfe wallet at thirty-nine pounds is a fair price for the material, but it is not a pick, because the brand's ethics claims rest on its own word and the brand is closing.

00:08:31.843 --> 00:08:43.555
One last point. Black Friday means nearly every brand is advertising discounts with no end dates. That says nothing about the clothes, but it means this was a quieter week for confirmed finds than usual.
